1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,340
I think Caribbean people, we don't realize our privilege.

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:07,020
We are very dismissive of the Black American experience.

3
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:08,640
Oh, you all just have a chip on your shoulder.

4
00:00:08,780 --> 00:00:10,540
You all need to just keep your head down and just work.

5
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:16,880
And it's like, yo, we grew up having Black teachers, Black politicians, Black leaders, Black doctors, Black dentists.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,020
Not to say we don't have colorism, classism, and versions of racism in the Caribbean.

7
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:30,220
But we are very privileged that we can walk in anywhere and we're the majority, 99.99999% of the time.

8
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:35,140
So we have a certain sense of security that no matter what, home is home.

9
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:38,520
But for me, coming to the U.S., when I go to New Orleans, I love that.

10
00:00:38,620 --> 00:00:39,860
I've been there like 15 times.

11
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,160
I will be there and New Orleans is like 70% Black and I feel like I love there.

12
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,640
But in some places, I'll be there and I'll just see the American flag.

13
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,160
I'll be like, yo, it just feels heavier, like it don't fit there.

